.TH "resources.yaml" "5" "Slurm Configuration File" "Slurm 26.11" "Slurm Configuration File"

.SH "NAME"
resources.yaml \-  Slurm configuration file for the Hierarchical Resources (HRES).

.SH "DESCRIPTION"

.LP
\fBresources.yaml\fR is a YAML\-formatted configuration file that defines
Hierarchical Resources (HRES).
The file must be located in the same directory as \fBslurm.conf\fR. Any text
following a "#" in this file is treated as a comment through the end of that
line.

Additional details are available in
the Hierarchical Resource (HRES) Scheduling page:
<https://slurm.schedmd.com/hres.html>

.LP

.SH "PARAMETERS"
.LP
Each Hierarchical Resources definition is a YAML object containing the following
attributes:
.LP

.TP
\fBresource\fR
The unique name for the resource. This name will be used to identify it by
slurmctld. This \fBmust be the first\fR attribute in the object.
.IP

.TP
\fBmode\fR
The planning mode for the resource. Must be one of the following:
.RS
.TP
\fBMODE_1\fR
Sufficient resources are required on \fBonly one\fR layer that overlaps with the
job's allocated nodes.
.TP
\fBMODE_2\fR
Sufficient resources must be available on \fBall\fR layers that overlap with the
job's allocated nodes.
.TP
\fBMODE_3\fR
Resources are allocated on a per-node basis. The scheduler calculates total
consumption from the most granular layer and sums it up into the successively
higher layers, ensuring the \fBcount\fR is not exceeded at any layer. This mode
is designed for consumable resources like power and can be mapped to a block
topology using the \fBtopology\fR parameter.
.RE
.IP

.TP
\fBtopology\fR
(Optional) Specifies the name of a \fBtopology/block\fR definition to map to
this resource. This parameter is \fBonly\fR valid for \fBMODE_3\fR resources.
The HRes layers must have the same layout as the specified block topology. This
enables the scheduler to simultaneously optimize both the HRes and block
topology allocations.
.IP

.TP
\fBvariables\fR
(Optional) A list of name/value pairs that map arbitrary text strings to
administratively-defined resource counts. Users can then use these strings in
job submissions (e.g., \fI--resources=power:full_node\fR) instead of a numeric
value. Each entry in the list contains:
.RS
.TP
\fBname\fR
The string alias for the value (e.g., "full_node").
.TP
\fBvalue\fR
The numeric resource count (e.g., 1000).
.RE
.IP

.TP
\fBdisable_hres\fR
(Optional) If true, no job is allocated resources from any layer of this
resource. Jobs may still request the resource, but they stay pending until it
is enabled again, with reason \fBHRESDisabled\fR. Running jobs keep the
resources that they already hold.
Defaults to false.
.IP

.TP
\fBlayers\fR
A list of layers that define the resource hierarchy. Each layer is a YAML object
containing:
.RS
.TP
\fBlayer_name\fR
The name of the layer, unique within the resource (case-insensitive).
.IP
.TP
\fBparent_name\fR
The \fBlayer_name\fR of the parent layer. Only valid for \fBMODE_3\fR, where it
is required for every layer except the root.
.IP
.TP
\fBnodes\fR
(Optional) A list of node names or hostlist expressions that are part of this
layer. Defaults to no nodes. In \fBMODE_3\fR, nodes may only be listed on leaf
layers; each parent layer is automatically assigned the nodes of its children.
.IP
.TP
\fBcount\fR
(Optional) The total number of resources available in this specific layer.
Defaults to 0. A value of \-1 means infinite.
.IP
.TP
\fBdisable_layer\fR
(Optional) If true, no job is allocated resources from this layer. Jobs may
still be allocated resources from the other layers of the same resource.
Defaults to false.
.IP
.TP
\fBbase\fR
(Optional) A list of name/value pairs describing non-job-related (static)
resource consumption in this layer. This is particularly relevant for
\fBMODE_3\fR. Each entry contains:
.RS
.TP
\fBname\fR
The name of the static resource consumer (e.g., "storage", "network1").
.TP
\fBvalue\fR
The numeric resource count this consumer uses.
.RE
.IP
.RE
.IP

.LP
The \fBnodes\fR, \fBcount\fR, \fBbase\fR, and \fBdisable_layer\fR of a layer,
and the \fBdisable_hres\fR of a resource, can be changed at runtime with the
\fBupdate HRESName\fR command of \fBscontrol\fR(1). Those changes are lost on
the next \fBscontrol reconfigure\fR or slurmctld restart, when this file is
read again.

.SH "EXAMPLE"
.nf
---
- resource: flat
  mode: MODE_2
  layers:
    - layer_name: top	# highest level
      nodes:
        - "node[01-32]"
      count: 24
    - layer_name: mid1	# middle levels
      nodes:
        - "node[01-16]"
      count: 16
    - layer_name: mid2
      nodes:
        - "node[17-32]"
      count: 16
    - layer_name: low1	# lowest levels
      nodes:
        - "node[01-08]"
      count: 12
    - layer_name: low2
      nodes:
        - "node[09-16]"
      count: 12
    - layer_name: low3
      nodes:
        - "node[17-24]"
      count: 12
    - layer_name: low4
      nodes:
        - "node[25-32]"
      count: 12
- resource: natural
  mode: MODE_1
  layers:
    - layer_name: top	# highest level
      nodes:
        - "node[01-32]"
      count: 50
    - layer_name: low1	# lowest levels
      nodes:
        - "node[01-16]"
      count: 100
    - layer_name: low2
      nodes:
        - "node[17-32]"
      count: 100
- resource: power
  mode: MODE_3
  topology: blok1
  variables:
    - name: full_node
      value: 1000
    - name: full_gpu_node
      value: 2000
  layers:
    - layer_name: site	# root, nodes inherited from children
      count: 130000
      base:
        - name: acUnit1
          value: 10000
        - name: acUnit2
          value: 8000
    - layer_name: row1
      parent_name: site
      count: 60000
      base:
        - name: network1
          value: 3000
    - layer_name: row2
      parent_name: site
      count: 80000
      base:
        - name: network2
          value: 2000
    - layer_name: rack1	# leaf layers
      parent_name: row1
      nodes:
        - "node[01-08]"
      count: 40000
      base:
        - name: storage
          value: 5000
    - layer_name: rack2
      parent_name: row1
      nodes:
        - "node[09-16]"
      count: 40000
    - layer_name: rack3
      parent_name: row2
      nodes:
        - "node[17-24]"
      count: 40000
    - layer_name: rack4
      parent_name: row2
      nodes:
        - "node[25-32]"
      count: 40000
.fi

